.TH V32IO.H 3v32 "October 14, 2026" "v32io 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
v32io.h \- v32io core: read a gamepad as a data channel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqv32io.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "v32io* v32io_init( int " gamepad " );"
.BI "void   v32io_free( v32io** " io " );"
.PP
.BI "bool   v32io_probe( v32io** " io " );"
.BI "int    v32io_read( v32io** " io " );"
.BI "int    v32io_changed( v32io** " io " );"
.BI "bool   v32io_isdown( v32io** " io ", int " mask " );"
.BI "bool   v32io_connected( v32io** " io " );"
.BI "bool   v32io_plugged( v32io** " io " );"
.BI "int    v32io_scan( v32io* " io " );"
.PP
.B #define V32IO_VERSION
.B #define V32IO_VERSION_NUMBER
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The v32io devices (a keyboard, a mouse...) are connected to the console
as ordinary gamepads. Their 11 controls (the D-pad, A, B, X, Y, L, R and
Start) carry data instead of game presses, and a driver in the program
decodes it. The core in
.B v32io.h
does the part that is the same for every device: it reads the 11
controls of one gamepad and packs them as the bits of a single integer,
the
.IR "packed controls" .
It knows nothing of what the bits mean. The drivers
.BR keyboard.h (3v32)
and
.BR mouse.h (3v32)
include it, so a program that uses them does not include
.B v32io.h
itself.
.PP
Like all Vircon32 libraries it is a single header with no library to link
against, and the compiler emits only the functions a program calls. It
includes
.BR input.h ", " misc.h " and " time.h
from the Vircon32 DevTools.
.SS Packed controls
Bit
.I n
holds the control read from port 0x402 + \fIn\fP. A bit is 1 while the
control is pressed:
.PP
.nf
bit  0  V32IO_LEFT      port 0x402  (INP_GamepadLeft)
bit  1  V32IO_RIGHT     port 0x403
bit  2  V32IO_UP        port 0x404
bit  3  V32IO_DOWN      port 0x405
bit  4  V32IO_START     port 0x406
bit  5  V32IO_A         port 0x407
bit  6  V32IO_B         port 0x408
bit  7  V32IO_X         port 0x409
bit  8  V32IO_Y         port 0x40A
bit  9  V32IO_L         port 0x40B
bit 10  V32IO_R         port 0x40C
.fi
.PP
.B V32IO_ALL
(2047) has all 11 bits set. Add the masks to test several controls at once.
.PP
The console never shows two opposite directions pressed at the same time
(Left with Right, or Up with Down): pressing one releases the other. A
device protocol has to be designed around that, and the ones in this family
are.
.SS Reading
All reading goes through the \fIprobe\fP, which a program calls
.B once on every frame
for each device. The probe reads the controls, and keeps the result of the
previous probe so that changes can be told. Calling it more than once in a
frame does nothing: the console updates its gamepads only between frames.
.SS Game ports
.B FIRST_GAMEPAD_PORT
(0),
.BR SECOND_GAMEPAD_PORT " (1),"
.BR THIRD_GAMEPAD_PORT " (2) and"
.B FOURTH_GAMEPAD_PORT
(3) are the ids that
.BR select_gamepad (3v32)
takes.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "v32io_init( int " gamepad )
Create an instance that reads the given gamepad, or return
.B NULL
if memory runs out. The instance holds a small machine code routine, built
in RAM, that reads the 11 ports in one call. Its starting state is whatever
the gamepad shows at that moment.
.TP
.BI "v32io_free( v32io** " io )
Release an instance and set the pointer to
.BR NULL .
.TP
.BI "v32io_probe( v32io** " io )
Read the controls for this frame. Returns true when the packed controls
differ from the previous probe. Does nothing, and returns false, if the
frame is the same as the last probe's.
.TP
.BI "v32io_read( v32io** " io )
The packed controls from the latest probe.
.TP
.BI "v32io_changed( v32io** " io )
The controls that changed in the latest probe, as a mask: the packed
controls of the latest and the previous probes, XORed.
.TP
.BI "v32io_isdown( v32io** " io ", int " mask )
True if any control in the mask is pressed (in the latest probe).
.TP
.BI "v32io_connected( v32io** " io )
Whether a gamepad is connected to the port (as of the latest probe).
.TP
.BI "v32io_plugged( v32io** " io )
True if the gamepad became connected in the latest probe. A newly connected
gamepad shows every control released whatever it showed before, so drivers
whose protocol depends on the history of the controls use this to start over.
.TP
.BI "v32io_scan( v32io* " io )
Read the controls right now and return them packed, without touching the
state of the probe. It selects the instance's gamepad for the read and then
restores the one selected before. Drivers use
.B v32io_probe
instead.
.PP
Every function that takes a
.I v32io**
accepts a pointer to
.B NULL
and then does nothing (returning 0 or false).
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
.B struct v32io
.nf
int   gamepad;     id of the gamepad being read
int   raw;         where the RAM routine stores its result
int   data;        packed controls, latest probe
int   previous;    packed controls, probe before that
int   frame;       frame counter at the latest probe
bool  connected;   gamepad connected, latest probe
bool  plugged;     gamepad became connected, latest probe
int [68] routine;  the RAM routine (V32IO_ROUTINE words)
.fi
.TP
.B V32IO_CONTROLS
11, the number of controls read.
.TP
.B V32IO_ROUTINE
68, the size of the RAM routine in words.
.TP
.B V32IO_VERSION
The version of the library as a string, such as
.BR \(dq20261014\-dev\(dq .
It is the release date as YYYYMMDD followed by \-dev or \-release.
.TP
.B V32IO_VERSION_NUMBER
The same date as a number (20261014), for comparisons at run time. The
Vircon32 C compiler has no
.B #if
so this is compared in ordinary code.
.PP
The version is stamped into the header by
.B make version
in the v32io project, which reads it from its
.B VERSION
file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count the frames a button of a gamepad on port 1 is held, by reading it
through the core directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "v32io.h"

void main()
{
    v32io* pad  = v32io_init( SECOND_GAMEPAD_PORT );
    int    held = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        v32io_probe( &pad );

        if( v32io_isdown( &pad, V32IO_A ) )
            held = held + 1;
        else
            held = 0;

        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The probe must run on every frame. A device that sends one piece of data
per frame loses it if the program skips frames, and the drivers cannot
tell.
.PP
The library allocates with
.BR malloc ,
so a program that needs it must leave memory for the instances (about 75
words for the core, and more for the driver on top of it).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR keyboard.h (3v32),
.BR mouse.h (3v32),
.BR v32io (3v32),
.BR input.h (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
